      "id": 1,
      "text": "Silver Peak was founded in 1863 after silver discoveries, making it one of Nevada’s oldest mining towns that still has residents.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/silver-peak-nevada-ghost-town/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The 1906 arrival of the Silver Peak Railroad linked the 100‑stamp Blair mill to the mines, making the region Nevada’s largest ore‑processing center of its era.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/silver-peak-nevada-ghost-town/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Silver Peak’s adobe buildings and stone ruins echo over 5 million ounces of silver mined, yet modern lithium operations prevent it from becoming a ghost town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/silver-peak-nevada-ghost-town/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Silver Peak experiences a cold desert climate, with chilly winters and scorching summers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Peak%2C_Nevada"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Silver Peak sits near a dry lake bed that is rich in lithium and other minerals.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Peak%2C_Nevada"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Blair’s 100‑stamp mill closed in 1915, and the town became a ghost town by 1920.",
